  "pages": [
    {
      "type": "patchouli:spotlight",
      "item": "theoreticalsciences:laser_interferometer",
      "title": "Laser Interferometer",
      "text": "Two long arms at right angles, a laser split between them, and mirrors that hang free. A passing gravitational wave stretches one arm and squeezes the other by a thousandth of a proton's width.$(br2)$(thing)Tier 3$() machine."
    },
    {
      "type": "patchouli:text",
      "text": "$(thing)Made on the Extreme Assembly Table$() (it is also a crafting table). The recipe pages at the end of this entry - and JEI - show the pattern."
    },
    {
      "type": "patchouli:text",
      "text": "Balance the laser power and the arms' finesse between shot noise and radiation-pressure noise; add isolation stages against the ground's shaking (other machines nearby make it worse; underground is quieter); cool the test masses to beat thermal noise. A black hole merger needs a signal-to-noise of 8 at h = 10\u207b\u00b2\u00b9; a neutron star merger, five times fainter, needs cold mirrors."
    },
